@page "/form-color"

<CatHead Title="Colour">
    Picking a colour: the platform's own picker in the field's chrome, a preset palette to choose
    from, and a spectrum picker for a reader who may choose any colour at all.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormColor.ColourField" Title="Colour field" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-color</code> on <code>&lt;input type="color"&gt;</code> gives it the shared control
        heights, the field border and the field focus ring, so it lines up beside a
        <code>.form-input</code>; <code>.form-input--sm</code> and <code>.form-input--lg</code> size it. Pair
        it with a text field for the value when the reader may need to type or paste one — the native
        picker's own entry is per-engine.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormColor.Swatches" Title="Preset palette" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.swatch-grid</code> of <code>&lt;button class="swatch"&gt;</code>, each carrying its colour
        as <code>--swatch</code> in its style attribute, because the colour is your data. The chosen one
        is <code>aria-pressed="true"</code>, drawn as a ring rather than a tint so it shows on any
        colour. <strong>Name every swatch</strong> with <code>aria-label</code>: a colour is not an
        accessible name, and a reader who cannot tell two greens apart needs the word.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormColor.Picker" Title="Spectrum picker" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For a reader who chooses any colour at all, not one of yours — a brand colour, a theme.
        <code>.form-color-picker</code> holds a <code>.form-color-area</code> for saturation and brightness,
        <code>.form-range--hue</code> and <code>.form-range--alpha</code> on native ranges, and a
        <code>.form-color-preview</code> beside your own field. The value lives in the
        <code>[data-color-value]</code> field: the script writes it as the reader drags or presses a key and
        dispatches its <code>input</code> and <code>change</code>, so bind the field and nothing else. Typing
        in it moves the thumbs, and on leaving it is rewritten as lower-case hex, eight digits below full
        opacity.
    </p>
    <p>
        The area is one tab stop: the arrows move its thumb by one per cent and by ten with Shift, Home and
        End go to no and full saturation, Page Up and Page Down to full and no brightness. Give it an
        <code>aria-label</code> and a <code>data-color-valuetext</code> in your own words — <code>{s}</code>,
        <code>{v}</code>, <code>{h}</code>, <code>{a}</code> and <code>{hex}</code> are filled in. Leave the
        alpha slider out for an opaque colour.
    </p>
    <p>
        The spectrum is the same in every theme and does not mirror under <code>dir="rtl"</code>: saturation
        grows to the right and the hue runs red to red left to right, as in every colour tool. In forced
        colours it keeps its own colours, and so do the thumbs.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormColor.PickerChannels" Title="RGB fields and opacity" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-color-format="rgb"</code> on the field writes <code>rgb(r g b / a)</code> instead of hex,
        and number inputs with <code>data-color-channel</code> — <code>r</code>, <code>g</code> and
        <code>b</code> from 0 to 255, <code>a</code> as a percentage — edit one channel each. Every field is
        kept on the same colour, and an <code>input type="color"</code> with <code>data-color-value</code>
        works as well, holding the colour without its opacity.
    </p>
</CatExample>
